use (main issue).**\nWrapping the column in a function makes the predicate non-sargable. An ordinary index on `users.email` can't be used, so the database scans all of `users` and applies `lower()` to every row. Pick one of these fixes:\n- **Expression index:** `CREATE INDEX idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));`. This works in Postgres, Oracle and MySQL 8+. The query stays as it is.\n- **Case-insensitive type or collation:** use `citext` in Postgres, or a `_ci` collation in MySQL or SQL Server. Then compare `u.email = 'a@example.org'` directly against a plain index.\n- **Normalize on write:** store emails lowercased, add a unique index on the column, and query `u.email = 'a@example.org'`. This is usually the cleanest option, and it also enforces uniqueness.\n\n**2. `SELECT *` pulls every column from both tables.**\n- It fetches unneeded data and rules out index-only scans.\n- It returns duplicate column names (`id` and `created_at`, for example), which can break or confuse clients.\n- It breaks silently when the schema changes.\n- List only the columns you need.\n\n**3. Check that `orders.user_id` is indexed.**\nOnce the user lookup is fast, the join needs an index on `orders(user_id)`. Otherwise it scans `orders` for each matching user. Foreign keys in Postgres and SQL Server don't create this index automatically.\n\n**4. No `LIMIT` or pagination.**\nA user with many orders returns an unbounded result set. Add `ORDER BY` with `LIMIT` if the caller only needs recent orders. A composite index such as `orders(user_id, created_at DESC)` would then cover both the join and the sort.\n\n**Suggested rewrite** (assuming emails are stored lowercased and indexed):\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.total, o.created_at, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE u.email = 'a@example.org'\nORDER BY o.created_at DESC\nLIMIT 50;\n```\n\nI only had the query text. I haven't seen the schema, the existing indexes, the row counts or an `EXPLAIN` plan, so the impact of each point is unconfirmed. If you share the DB engine and the output of `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE)`, I can confirm which of these actually matters.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}